 17592186232044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231877 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231877 #edb/ref 1061 629573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231877 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "241e6b5c-c0f1-5f23-a1c7-c370cd5c8fa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231877 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231878 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231878 #edb/ref 1061 629576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231878 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a192e4a9-8faa-5a1b-93df-775720b2cded" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231878 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186232045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231879 #edb/ref 1061 629577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231879 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71ad2797-2e06-5afb-b5a7-eec5050eac95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231879 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232043 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned that $\\max$ and $\\min$ return the largest and smallest *function values,* while $\\arg\\max$ and $\\arg\\min$ return the corresponding *input values.*\n\nIn this lesson, we practice finding $\\max,$ $\\arg\\max,$ $\\min,$ and $\\arg\\min$ from common representations of a function:\n\n- Tables of values\n\n- Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232043 #edb/ref 1065 15116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232043 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a3e684e6-ccf3-5429-8f42-d25353c5ef2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232043 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232044 #edb/ref 1064 "When a table lists input-output pairs $(x,f(x))$ for $x$ in some set $S,$ we can read:\n\n- $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by finding the largest output value in the table.\n\n- $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by taking the input value where that largest output occurs.\n\nThe same idea applies to $\\min$ and $\\arg\\min,$ except we look for the smallest output value." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232044 #edb/ref 1065 15117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9668d165-c819-5345-ae0d-924ef3496969" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232044 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding Argmax and Argmin From a Table" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232045 #edb/ref 1064 "When a graph shows the points $(x,f(x))$ for $x$ in a set $S,$ we can find:\n\n- $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by locating the highest point on the graph and reading its $y$-coordinate.\n\n- $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ by taking the corresponding $x$-coordinate.\n\nThe same idea applies to $\\min$ and $\\arg\\min,$ except we look for the lowest point." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232045 #edb/ref 1065 15118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232045 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "874a2ec9-3803-59fe-9157-7b451a113671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232045 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding Argmax and Argmin From a Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232316 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186232317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232316 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232316 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232316 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186232317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232316 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "664dfe98-66e6-5bae-a9a7-b47a5a5e45f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232317 #edb/ref 1080 "a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232317 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232317 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19156b0d-85dd-55bf-92c5-90b05ca8405a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186232319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232318 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232318 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232318 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186232319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232318 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f2a16269-c3d6-50fd-9b87-ae55cd7d3020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232319 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232319 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232319 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e8d59528-68c8-562f-b103-349c4f60afa6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232320 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186232321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232320 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232320 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232320 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186232321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232320 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1082e8b5-8913-5875-8fe3-ad51c1835a69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232321 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232321 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232321 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8aad39ba-6db2-56df-800c-230c6458882c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232322 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186232323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232322 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232322 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232322 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186232323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232322 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "35d8314b-9f95-5b94-bfab-fca783b713e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232323 #edb/ref 1080 "7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232323 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232323 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42ca0cda-c134-5297-8632-955cb72a1ca9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2d/2d6d392d4e0f773f962289075ffa07cd26353ebc00ec50e7473a5c992824417f.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the solid shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "552d9e5c-d038-53c1-b922-9832413afdda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233094 #edb/ref 1074 "The solid is a **right cylinder**. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has two parallel circular bases. So, it's a cylinder.\n\n- The axis that passes through the centers of the circles is perpendicular to the bases. So, it's right." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233209 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114209" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233209 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233209 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233209 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1df5e375-31b2-566a-aee3-e06ed27bbd47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233209 #edb/ref 1074 "We split the integral into the sum of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0)+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)+\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233216 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233216 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233216 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d6/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe914836-cad9-5ede-b4ee-c13fc7ae4032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233216 #edb/ref 1074 "The major axis is the longest diameter of the ellipse, which is the line segment bounded by the points $(-3,1)$ and $(5,1).$ So, the length of the major axis is $5-(-3)=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233277 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233277 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233277 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233277 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233277 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6dd4ef9-dcc4-5324-91b6-a18f8265b17f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233277 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462856 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186233427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462857 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186233427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462858 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186233427 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233427 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233427 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bc/bc3809e55e601738d56be6885475ed442dec1fb3a0d2e19da14be045510f73dc.png)\n\nThe above scatter plot shows the relationship between age and years of experience of a group of professional soccer players. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Only one player has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\nII. There are two players aged $21.$\n\nIII. None of the players has fewer than $6$ years of experience.\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186233427 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c285e0d-9ca2-5d71-ae56-cfc30b5c536e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233427 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $10,$ namely, $(21,10).$ This means only one of the players has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\n![](images/20/209759f5d70fd8587740d1da840f9da838a3c9a055a0d1e962f76c1cd6341fd3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $21,$ namely, $(21,10)$ and $(21,12).$ This means that two players are $21$ years old.\n\n![](images/75/753c503d6751363b1c7d2dc449f1c5f11baec159b1e938e8c807938512111750.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, one player has fewer than $6$ years of experience. The dot below the horizontal line represents this player.\n\n![](images/66/66facd1a0683f420aa2ac3d7bfd54290bdb009850bc66cc7523ee569ec81daea.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233458 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233458 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233458 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233458 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(-\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(3\\sqrt{3},5\\sqrt{3})?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233458 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20c93a82-e610-599d-874a-ddfeea4ed232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233458 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have ${{(-\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(3\\sqrt{3},5\\sqrt{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3\\sqrt{3}-(-\\sqrt{3}))}^{2}+{(5\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16(3)+9(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48+27} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1071 "If the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(7,-4)$ and $(a,b)$ is $(2,1),$\nthen $a+b=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd717350-e4af-57c0-a511-28912f1d1ece" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233494 #edb/ref 1074 "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1 &=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a &=-3 \\\\ b &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186233501 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233501 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233501 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233501 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation of a curve is $xy+{x}^{2}=1.$ The normal line at the point $(1,\\,0)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233501 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ada3b28b-b3a4-5dcc-bddd-f6c1a31f531f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233501 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(1,\\,0).$   Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2x & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(2x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,0)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2x+y}{x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+0}{1} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(1,\\,0)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1071 "Peter has $8$ shirts, $6$ pants, and $6$ pairs of shoes. If Peter wears one shirt, one pant, and one pair of shoes, how many different ways can Peter dress?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9db0cb85-c477-5f32-9094-207566837840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233616 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of outfits} & =\\text{Number of shirts} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pants} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pairs of shoes} \\\\  & =8\\times 6\\times 6 \\\\  & =288.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Peter can dress in $288$ different ways." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233624 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233624 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233624 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+8$ is strictly increasing." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46f89f9b-6749-5057-8b7c-a30df75a08ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233624 #edb/ref 1074 "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6x(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=6x(x-1)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/ec/ec7ea6baa82d60a6cf472c904460190042d889d86cb092b19d9809111bd93b36.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233750 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233750 #edb/ref 1069 "q-29440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233750 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233750 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/51/51a05d6b2fe04eb825b8048281b6d1e94b65df9f6f80c6e8965f9e414c5b360c.png)\n\nFind the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233750 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0007b86f-ed50-58f6-ba24-2a509c62a4ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233750 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$\n\n- The line intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,1)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=1.$\n\n- The line passes through the points $(0,1)$ and $(1,-1)$, so its slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{-1-1}{1-0}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=-2x+1.$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is *below* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<-2x+1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234156 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/15/15f17827141a0a31a7b748ab93c6811afcebd8c66781642554814f126aab8add.png)\n\nWhat is the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55f623fe-9f1f-5ecf-9326-8e25231b1f53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234156 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$\n\n- The line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-1)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=-1.$\n\n- The line passes through the points $(0,-1)$ and $(2,0)$, so its slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{0-(-1)}{2-0}=\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=\\frac{x}{2}-1.$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is solid, we will use an \"or equal to\" inequality. Since the shaded region is *above* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge \\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1071 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has ${a}_{4}=13$ and ${a}_{8}=41,$ what is the index of the term $90?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51d856f6-bec1-5419-b978-851c105050a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234235 #edb/ref 1074 "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term ${a}_{1}.$\n\nSince we are given ${a}_{4}=13$ and ${a}_{8}=41,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{4}}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{41-13}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{4} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{4}=13$ is $3$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}+3(7) \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}+21 \\\\ -8 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-8+(n-1)(7) \\\\  & =-8+7n-7 \\\\  & =7n-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=90,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =7n-15 \\\\ 90 & =7n-15 \\\\ 105 & =7n \\\\ \\frac{105}{7} & =n \\\\ 15 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $90$ is the $15$th term of the sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234327 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234327 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234327 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234327 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234327 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73ac4960-30ae-5f42-9db9-54e717e4f3da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234327 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $9!=9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!$ and $6!=6\\cdot 5!\\,.$ Then, we can cancel the $7!$ and $5!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!} & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!\\cdot 5!}{7!\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{7!}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{6} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234406 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234406 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234406 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234406 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the equation of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}=40$ in standard form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234406 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74bac2ee-5e85-545f-90a2-096a6f681023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234406 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $40$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{40}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{40}{40} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236127 #edb/ref 1080 "False" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236127 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236127 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "308b89d9-5d11-59d7-be97-d10de51eb1b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236295 #edb/ref 1080 "True" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236295 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236295 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a90c3d1-f87f-5dd4-8d1a-852847527935" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237115 #edb/ref 1080 "Often" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237115 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237115 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cc79f0fa-9b89-59ca-bf18-3fa0b6b3dc96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237173 #edb/ref 1080 "Sometimes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237173 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237173 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d65830b8-a74d-509d-b4b9-54ce1fb82f24" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
